<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assembly of the Year XIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Assembly of Year XIII</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assembly of the Year XIII was the general constituent assembly of the United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, which met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> from 1813. Although it did not declare independence, it adopted a national anthem and coat of arms, abolished noble titles, the Inquisition and torture, and declared children born to enslaved mothers free. Its rejection of the delegates from the Eastern Province deepened the split with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assembly-of-the-year-xiii.jpg" alt="Assembly of the Year XIII" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asamblea_del_a%C3%B1o_XIII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The assembly, formally the General Constituent and Sovereign Assembly of the Year 1813, was convened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a>. Its decree of 24 October 1812 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> four deputies, each provincial capital two and each dependent city one, with Tucumán allowed two as a reward for the recent victory over the royalist army. Deputies came from the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> and Mizque were represented after elections held under the protection of the Auxiliary Army, while those chosen for Santa Cruz de la Sierra and Cochabamba never took their seats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At its opening session on 31 January 1813 the assembly declared that it held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the people and that its purpose was to draft a constitution, with Carlos de Alvear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a> as president and Valentín Gómez and Hipólito Vieytes as secretaries. It sat in five periods until January 1815. The deputies of the Banda Oriental, chosen at the Congress of Tres Cruces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a>, were rejected on 1 June 1813, a decision confirmed on the 11th; Uruguayan historians traditionally hold that the real reason was Artigas's instructions for immediate independence and a confederation.</p><p>Besides the reforms for which it is best known, the assembly made 25 May a civic holiday, removed the image of Ferdinand VII from coins and documents, ordered the first national coins in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> at the Potosí Mint, proclaimed freedom of the press, and abolished the personal service of the Indians under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a>, the mit'a and the yanaconazgo. At the start of 1814 it replaced the Triumvirate with the Directory, a single Supreme Director, electing Gervasio Antonio de Posadas, who governed with little reference to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The assembly last met on 26 January 1815 and did not meet again under the Directory of Carlos María de Alvear. It was formally dissolved after the Revolution of the Fontezuelas of 18 April 1815, a coup by the troops under Colonel Ignacio Álvarez Thomas that had been sent against Artigas, backed by a popular rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. Argentina marked its bicentenary with a one-time national holiday on 31 January 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assembly of the Year XIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Assembly of the Year XIII</b> reshaped society by law: the Law of Wombs made the children of slaves free, any slave entering the territory was freed, the mit'a, encomienda and yanaconazgo were abolished and Indigenous people declared citizens with equal rights, noble ranks disappeared, and the wealthy…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Within a year the <b>Assembly of Year XIII</b> judged that song not effective enough, and the army's recent victories had made its words out of date.</blockquote><a href="/songs/argentine-national-anthem">Argentine National Anthem</a></li></ul></section>
